EldenNet, the read-only round (package @LABEL@)
@UNDERLINE@

This package runs a read-only round of EldenNet's address library (Lodestar; EldenNet's decisions O100 to O102) on
your own PC. The two offline runs you made read the game's executable file and found the 217 places where the game
keeps its main objects (EldenNet calls them managers: one keeps the world's characters, another the time of day, and
so on), without knowing which is which. This round starts the game with EldenNet loaded and reads, from
the game as it runs, which of those places holds which of the eleven managers EldenNet needs. Then it tries, for a few
minutes, the way EldenNet v1 will read the game: from a thread of its own, about ten times a second.

This is the twenty-first round. The twentieth worked at its third run: its report came whole, and with your status
screen beside it the places of your character's sheet could be named. Your position, a character's type and the block
of the map it is in are known places now. It also showed that the game does not mark your own death as it marks an
enemy's, and it left three things without their second reading: the time of day, the play time and the count of your
deaths. That was the round's fault, not yours: its background watch got almost none of the reader's time.

This round is after five things.
  1. The time of day and the play time, read by a second reader beside EldenNet's own. That needs time, so the last
     task is four minutes of playing as you like.
  2. The count of your deaths: the one number of the save data that rises by one, once, when you die.
  3. How the game marks your own death: by your health at nothing, and whether one bit we suspect goes with it.
  4. Your level and your name through EldenNet's own reader, as v1 will read them. Neither is written (see below).
  5. Your wish of 27 November: the FOCUS and the STAMINA of a character, as it is now and at its most. Your sheet
     has them (the last round); a character's own part, which enemies and NPCs have too, is where EldenNet wants them.

THE PANEL TELLS YOU WHAT IT GATHERED, as before. When a task is done, the panel says under it "Got it." or "Not
gathered.", a second or two later. When all are done it says, at its foot and in the console, either "This run is
usable: send it." or which tasks were not gathered. You never need to start again: whatever happens on the way
(attacked, killed sooner than you meant), carry on and send the files. "Not gathered" may be the round's fault and not
yours. For the last task the panel's verdict now rests on what the report file holds as written, not on what was read.

The tasks, which you agreed to on 27 November. Load your usual character somewhere quiet, with no enemy near, and get
off your horse. Stand still for 20 seconds. Then find any enemy and let it kill you, once; wait until you are back in
the world and can move, and press Pause. Then stay in the world for four minutes and play as you like: ride, fight,
explore. In those four minutes, now and then SPRINT OR ROLL, AND USE A SPELL OR A WEAPON SKILL, so that your stamina
and your focus go down and come back: that is how the round tells them from every other number. Do not quit to the
title in that time, and do not sit long at a grace. Dying again does no harm. Then quit.

BEFORE YOU START: dying drops the runes you carry. Spend them first, or die where they are easy to pick up again, and
do not die a second time before you have picked them up.

New reading, which you agreed to on 27 November: the first 512 bytes of the part of your own character that holds its
health, at each look of the sheet scan (every two seconds or a little slower). The round holds each 32-bit field of it
against six numbers of your sheet (health, focus and stamina, each as it is now and at its most) and writes, for a
field that read the same as one of them in nine looks of ten: where the field lies, in how many looks it read the same,
and in how many the two changed together. NO VALUE of it is written.

Also new: EldenNet's own reader reads your sheet once a second through its table (its first 192 bytes), as v1 will.
It takes your level, and it looks at the letters of your character's name in memory to count them. It writes neither
the level, nor a letter, nor the count: only in how many looks a level and a name were there, and in how many each had
changed since the look before. The letters are wiped from the reader's copy at once; the count is kept in memory
only until the next look, to see whether it changed.

As in the last round, by your choice then: the sheet scan reads the 64 KiB from where the save data's object begins
and the 64 KiB from where your sheet begins, and writes NUMBERS OF YOUR CHARACTER: for every 32-bit number from 1 to
99,999,999 there that changes rarely or never, where it lies, what it read first and last, in how many looks it
changed, and in which of your tasks. New beside it: for every field that rose by exactly one, exactly once (whatever
it holds, a number beside a text or a large one too), where it lies and in which task; no value. Your character's name
lies in that sheet: the scan copies those bytes with the rest, recognises text by its shape, in any script, and writes
nothing of it, only where a run of text lies and how many fields long it is. One case its shape cannot catch: a name
of ONE SINGLE LETTER looks like a small number. If the character you load has such a name, do not run this round, and
tell me.

The report goes from you to me and no further: no number of your character goes into the repository, the records, a
message to another session or any page. As before it also holds your health, your maximum health and the save's slot
as they stood just after each task, and never where you stand.

AFTERWARDS, in the chat: nothing is needed this time. If your status screen has changed since the last one you sent
(a level gained), a new screenshot helps; it stays in the chat.

The game may again end with an error code when you quit. That code is the game's own on your PC, it does not show
every time, and we do not know what decides it. If it shows, the launcher keeps its record quietly and says
nothing at its next start.

The other reading is as before, at the same careful pace: in the background, as since the seventh round, memory near
the event flags' own object (about 24 MiB in all at most), the flags' reader, the clock and the play time; once a
second EldenNet's reader on your character, about 2 KiB a time, and every other second on the characters near you
(about 24 KiB); and the survey of the last three rounds (the first 2 KiB of each of up to 48 characters near you and
of your own, every two seconds or a little slower, and every ten seconds from twenty seconds after your death on).
The watch on all the parts of your character, which the last round ran, does not run in this one. The background
watch now also reads, once a second, the first 512 bytes of the clock's object and of the save data's object: the
same two objects EldenNet's reader reads, by a second reader. If the game stutters, quit it and tell us.

It only reads. It calls nothing in the game, adds nothing to it and changes nothing in it. The panel is a window of
the launcher's own, outside the game. Nothing in this package is for playing with yet.

Built from EldenNet's engine branch at commit @COMMIT@ with clang-cl (Release). Never shared: a test build for you
alone, labelled @LABEL@.


How long it takes
-----------------

About 15 minutes: 5 to unzip and start, 1 at the title screen, then about 7 for finding a quiet spot, the death and
the four minutes of play, and a few to send the files. The panel keeps the time for you.


What is in it
-------------

  read.cmd                Double-click this to start the round.
  eldennet-launcher.exe   EldenNet's launcher: backs up your saves, starts the game offline without Easy Anti-Cheat,
                          loads EldenNet, and watches the game until it ends.
  eldennet.dll            EldenNet in the game: the probe build, which runs this round and nothing else.
  eldennet-engine.exe     EldenNet's command-line tool, here to check the report (its lint command).
  gal\                    What the round works from: the build's fingerprint (builds.toml), how a manager's place is
                          recognised (recipes.toml, with its note in derivations\), EldenNet's own numbers and labels
                          for the places it needs (ids.toml), what to look for (leads.toml), and the table of
                          places the rounds have found so far for your game's build (layouts\). Numbers and names
                          of the design's own, nothing taken from the game.
  README-READ.txt         This file.
  SHA256SUMS              The SHA-256 of every other file in this package.
  LICENSE.txt             EldenNet's licence (MIT).
  THIRD_PARTY.md          What EldenNet is built with, and its credits.
  THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt The licences of the libraries inside the programs.


Before you start
----------------

  - Close the game, and start Steam.
  - Unzip this package into a folder of its own, anywhere but the game's folder.
  - The launcher backs up your saves before it starts the game (in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\save-backups), and the round
    does not run without that backup. If you want a copy of your own as well, copy the folder %APPDATA%\EldenRing
    somewhere first.
  - The game will run offline, without Easy Anti-Cheat. Use a save you are happy to play offline, and never take a save
    played with mods into online play.
  - Set the game to windowed or borderless windowed mode first (in its graphics settings): the panel is a window of
    its own and a full-screen game covers it. It sits at the top right of the screen the game is on; drag it by its
    top line if it is in the way.
  - The launcher's messages also appear in a console window behind the game, as before.
  - Windows or your security software may warn about an unsigned program. Allow it only if the SHA-256 values match
    (below); otherwise stop and tell us.


The steps
---------

1. Double-click read.cmd. (Or, in a command prompt in this folder: eldennet-launcher.exe --read --read-plan 3)

   The first time, the launcher asks where eldenring.exe is if it cannot find it through Steam, and shows its notices.

2. The game starts. At the title screen, wait. The console says, in lines starting with "EldenNet read:", that it is
   reading the game. This takes about a minute. Do not load a save until it tells you to.

3. When the round is ready, the panel's first task turns grey. From then on follow the panel. Its tasks, in order:

     1. Wait at the title screen                  (the round does this one itself)
     2. Load your usual character                 go somewhere quiet, with no enemy near, and get off your horse;
                                                  press Pause when you stand there, then let go of the controls
     3. Stand still, 20 s                         on foot; hands off the keyboard and the mouse
     4. Let an enemy kill you, once               any enemy; wait until you are back in the world and can move,
                                                  then press Pause. If you died sooner than you meant to, that
                                                  counts too: carry on
     5. Stay in the world, 4 min                  play as you like, but stay in the world: no quitting, no long
                                                  stop at a grace. Now and then sprint or roll, and use a spell
                                                  or a weapon skill
     6. Quit the game

   Take your time over task 2: nothing is counted until you press Pause. A run in which you are attacked or
   killed before task 4 still counts: carry on with the tasks as they come, and send the files whatever the
   panel says at the end. Under each done task the panel says "Got it." or "Not gathered." a second or two
   after it ends; when all are done, its foot and the console say whether the run is usable.

   The waiting tasks count down and end by themselves. A task of yours waits until you press Pause (anywhere, while
   the game has the keyboard) or click the panel's button, which says what it stands for. The last task has no
   button: quitting the game ends it. The task under way is highlighted; done tasks are grey, with the time they
   took.

   Two things matter. Press Pause only once the game is really there (standing at your spot; back in the world
   after the death and able to move), and during a waiting task do what it says until its time is up: that is
   when the round looks.

4. Quit the game as you usually do. The console then says whether the two files passed their check, and the launcher
   ends.

5. Send these files back to us:

     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe\lodestar-found.json   required
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe\lodestar-table.toml   required
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\logs\eldennet.log           helpful
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\logs\launcher.log           helpful
     %APPDATA%\EldenNet\crashes\                    the newest file there, if the game ended with an error
                                                    code (the launcher keeps a short record of it: see the top
                                                    of this file)

   (Type %APPDATA%\EldenNet into Explorer's address bar to open the folder.) You can check the report yourself:

     eldennet-engine.exe lint --report "%APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe\lodestar-found.json"

   It must say "lint: the report is clean". The launcher has checked it the same way already.

If anything else happens (the console says the build is not supported, or that the round stopped, or the game closes),
do not try again: send the two logs and tell us what the console said. That is a useful result too.


What it does
------------

  - Backs up your saves (it only copies them), checks that Steam is running and that the game is not, and checks that
    your game is the build the offline runs read (2.7.2.0, worldwide). If the launcher says the build is not
    supported, stop: the game has been updated since, and the offline run must be made again first.
  - Starts the game offline without Easy Anti-Cheat, by writing the game's Steam app id into steam_appid.txt beside
    the game while it starts, and removing it again (eldennet-launcher.exe --cleanup removes it if a crash left it).
  - Loads eldennet.dll into the game. In the game, EldenNet reads the game's code and data as they are loaded,
    read-only and through guarded copies, and finds again the 217 places the offline runs found. It also looks
    through the code for four other ways the same fetch of a manager is written (gal\recipes.toml lists them, each
    with its note); the third round counted them: 1,279 more places, which lead to the same managers and to two
    places more, which hold nothing EldenNet asks for.
  - For each of those places it reads the address kept there and, where there is one, the first eight bytes of the
    object at that address, once. Those eight bytes say what kind of object it is, and the game's own type
    information, already read from its code, gives that kind a class. Where the class is one of the eleven EldenNet
    asks for, the place is that manager's. For the world's clock, a kind whose name holds the expected one counts
    too, marked as weaker until a later round confirms it. It does this again every ten seconds, for as long as any
    of the eleven is still missing.
  - From a thread of its own, about ten times a second, it reads each named manager's place and the first eight bytes
    of its object again, and counts: how often the place was empty, how often the object was still of the same kind,
    how often a read was refused, and how long a reading took. This is the way EldenNet v1 will read the game, tried
    here on sixteen bytes a manager.
  - The panel: a small window of the launcher's own (not of the game), always on top, that never takes the keyboard.
    The launcher asks Windows for the Pause key while the round runs (a system hotkey, as in the earlier rounds:
    Pause goes to the launcher and not to the game meanwhile) and gives it back at the end. When you confirm a
    task of yours, or a standing or looking task's time is up, the launcher writes the moment into the small
    block of memory it shares with EldenNet, and the round reads it there. The launcher also writes there, before
    the game starts, which list of tasks this round follows (read.cmd names it: --read-plan 3). And the round
    writes there, for the panel, two bits for each task that ends with a mark: whether what the task was for
    was gathered. Nothing else passes between the two.
  - From the same thread, every two seconds, the watch: it follows the way to each named manager's object and
    copies its first 512 bytes (fewer where the object is smaller). From one copy to the next it counts, for every
    eight bytes, whether they changed and whether they rose, and now and then what they look like: nothing, a small
    number, a kind of object, an address in the game's code or data, an address of memory in use, or something
    else. It also counts, for every run of six bits, whether it behaves as a clock's minutes do (never 60 or more,
    stepping forward and going round), for every run of five whether it behaves as hours (never 24 or more,
    stepping by one), and for every four bytes whether, read as a fraction or as a whole number, they keep rising
    at a steady pace; and when such hours or such a number jump instead (a rise far above the pace, or a fall), it
    keeps the first three jumps: after how many seconds, and the number before and after. Beside the managers it
    watches: the objects the event flags' first addresses lead to (eight at most) and the objects those lead to
    (four each at most); the object where the play time is kept; 16,384 bytes of the object of the clock's kind and
    of the manager that points at it; and, once your save is loaded and every three seconds (less often where
    nothing ever changes), the object behind each of the other known places, for a clock only: where minutes go
    round twice with hours beside them, the place is written into the table as the world's clock. It writes counts
    and offsets, and for a number that jumped the number before and after each of its first three jumps (the
    clock's own reading, if it is the clock): nothing else the memory held.
  - From the same thread, the sweep: from each address the event flags' own object keeps (eight at most) it copies
    up to 2 MiB of memory, and from each address one step further (32 at most) up to 256 KiB, a piece of 16,384
    bytes at a time, and compares each piece with the copy before. Where one single bit of a byte has changed, in
    eight bytes that otherwise keep still, it writes where (the offset from the address), which bit, whether it
    went up or down, and after how many seconds: never what the memory holds. The bits that changed from 15 seconds
    before one of your confirmations to a minute after it are kept under that confirmation. A pass through all of
    it is followed by a rest, so that a pass starts every thirty seconds at most, and one starts at once when you
    confirm. A stretch of which more than a thousand places change again and again is read no more.
  - Once your save is loaded, from the same thread, three searches, each of which only reads:
      - For a manager whose kind of object the game has but which none of those places holds: it goes through the
        game's own data for another place that points at an object of that kind, reading the first eight bytes of
        each object it finds a pointer to, three times at most. Where several places point at one and the same
        object, the manager is named by the one the game's code refers to most.
      - For the same managers: it copies the first 16,384 bytes of the object behind each of the places, and looks
        there for an object of the missing kind, or for a pointer to one (eight bytes read behind each pointer).
        What it finds, the watch then follows.
      - For the play time: over the objects the game's data points at, it looks for a number that rises by the
        clock while you play. The place it finds goes into the table, with the places in the game's code that refer
        to it; the watch then follows the object, and this search ends.
    The searches and the watch follow pointers that may no longer be good. So the round first asks Windows, for
    every number in the game's data that could be an address, whether there is memory there that may be read
    (VirtualQuery, which reads nothing), and keeps only those; 96 in 100 of them are no address at all. Before each
    read it asks again, and leaves the memory alone if the answer has changed; then it copies with Windows' own
    function for reading a program's memory (ReadProcessMemory, on the game itself), which answers "cannot be read"
    without disturbing the game. Such a read takes about half a thousandth of a second on your PC, so the round
    looks at its clock after every one: together they take 4 thousandths of a second of each step of EldenNet's own
    thread, give or take one read; the game's own threads are never used. What they find is written as counts and
    places, never as what the memory held.
  - From the same thread, once, the tree: from the address the event flags' own object keeps where the seventh round
    saw records of three addresses each, it reads one record of 64 bytes, then the records that one points at, and
    so on, each once, 32,768 at most. For each of a record's sixteen numbers it counts over all records how many
    different values it takes and how many are multiples of a block's size. Then, for each bit that changed around
    one of your confirmations, it looks for the record that names that bit's block, and writes that record's
    numbers (the group's number among them; an address is written as 0), and whether the way down the tree by
    that number arrives at the same record. Nothing else of the records is written. Of the flags' own object it
    reads the first 256 bytes once and writes only where in them the numbers 1,000 and 125 stand.
  - EldenNet's own reader, from the same thread, every ten seconds: for the flag that is set in every playthrough
    and for each flag the round has numbered from a bit you changed, it reads the flags' own object, goes down the
    tree by the flag's group (up to sixteen or so records of 64 bytes), and reads the one byte that holds the
    flag. It writes each such flag's number, whether it read as set, and whether that agrees with what the round
    saw. Before that, once, it looks through the game's code as loaded for the places the table names and checks
    them (the same reading as the round's first pass).
  - From the same thread, from your first confirmation on, every two seconds until the game ends, the character
    survey: it reads your character's address and the two ends of the list of characters near you from the
    character manager (24 bytes), the list's first 48 elements (768 bytes), and of your character and each of
    those the first 2,048 bytes of its object (less where memory ends sooner) and, through the holder of its
    parts, its health (two reads of 8 bytes). It follows each character over the session by its address (128 at
    most), compares what it read with what it read two seconds before, and counts. It writes, for a 32-bit number
    that keeps still in nearly every character: where it lies, how many different values the characters hold, the
    largest group that shares one, how many are nothing, small, an address, shaped as an area's number or as large
    as an id, and whether yours stands alone; where eight bytes are different in every character; where one number
    is another divided by 10,000; where a bit flipped as a character's health went to nothing, and for how many
    living and dead characters that bit then stood the same way; and where a bit flipped while you looked around
    and not while you stood. And for twenty-one checks of the places the earlier rounds left open (two candidates for a
    character's type, the block it is in and two candidates for the map it belongs to, the dead bit and its two
    rivals) it counts, for your character and for the others apart, in how many looks each held: a place reading
    nothing, or the number the design expects of a creature of the world, or the same as another place, or the
    same as that place reads for you, or a number shaped as a block's, or another number than at the look before;
    or two bits reading alike. It never writes what a number held, of your character or any other.
  - EldenNet's own reader on your character, from the same thread, once a second, through the table of places for
    your game's build (gal\layouts): the address of the game's state's object and the save's slot in it (4
    bytes); from the character manager, your character's address and the two ends of the list of characters near
    you (32 bytes); the first eight bytes of your character's object, to see that it is of the player's kind, then
    its first 496 bytes, the first 112 bytes of the holder of its parts, the first 320 bytes of the part with
    your health and the first 128 bytes of the part with your position; and of the list of characters near you
    the first 48 elements (768 bytes) with the first eight bytes of each one's object. About 2 KiB a second.
    Every other second, as in the last round: of each of those 48 that is a character and not your own, the first
    496 bytes of its object (about 24 KiB), from which it takes the fields the table places: the handle, the
    kind, the model, the map's id, the side, the dead bit, the on-screen bit, and now the type and the block.
    Every second, as in the last round: the world's clock (the first 32 bytes of its object) and the play time
    (the first 168 bytes of the save data's object), through the table. New in this round, every second: your
    sheet through the table (its first 192 bytes), for your level and for the length of your name, of which
    it writes only in how many looks each was there and in how many it had changed. It
    writes: how many times each of these was there to be read; how long the list was, and for how many of its
    elements a character lay behind the address and a plain distance beside it; for each stretch between two of
    your confirmations, in how many seconds your position had moved more than a centimetre since the second
    before, your health read nothing, your character was there, its dead bit was set, and its block was
    another than in the look before; in how many looks the clock's minute had stepped on by one, stood, or
    jumped, and the play time had risen; and, as it stood just after each of your confirmations and tasks:
    whether a save was loaded and in which slot, whether your character was there, YOUR HEALTH AND YOUR MAXIMUM
    HEALTH, and the length of the list. Of the characters' fields it writes counts only, as in the last round,
    with the type and the block counted the same way: how many characters had a block shaped as one, how many
    were in your block, how many types there were and how many characters of the commonest. It never writes
    the position itself, your block's number, or any of those numbers of other characters. Where the table
    lacks one of these places the reader reads nothing of it and says which.
  - The sheet scan, as in the last round, from the same thread, from your first confirmation on, every two seconds or a
    little slower until the game ends: your character's address from the character manager (8 bytes), the sheet's
    address from your character's object (8 bytes), then up to 64 KiB of memory from where the save data's object
    begins and up to 64 KiB from where the sheet begins, in pieces of 16 KiB (less where memory ends sooner). It
    compares each 32-bit field with what it read there the time before. It writes, for every field whose number is
    between 1 and 99,999,999 at the first or the last look and changed in eight looks at most: where it lies, THE
    NUMBER AS FIRST READ AND AS LAST READ, in how many looks it changed, and in which of your tasks. Left out and only
    counted: a number that changed more often (a timer), half of an address, and anything that reads as text. For text
    (two letters or more in a row, as the game stores them, in any script) it writes where the run lies and how many
    fields it covers, never a letter; a number standing right after a text, a fraction or an id is dropped with it,
    and so is a number of two million or more whose lower half could be a letter. It also writes whether the sheet is
    the object the save data's own words lead to. Two thousand numbers an object at most, from its start. New in
    this round: for every field that rose by exactly one in exactly one look, whatever it holds, where it lies and
    in which task (256 an object at most), never its value. And, just before each look at the sheet: the address of
    the holder of your character's parts (8 bytes), the address of the part with your health (8 bytes) and that
    part's first 512 bytes, held against six numbers of the sheet as said at the top of this file: places and
    counts, never a value.
  - The survey, as in the last three rounds, with two things added. For three bits of a character's object: in how
    many looks the bit was set while the character's health read nothing, for your character and for the others
    apart. And how many of the characters near you hold a sheet of the same kind as yours, of another kind, or none
    (your wish for a level of NPCs and enemies): counts only.
  - The background watch on the clock's object and the save data's object, once a second, 512 bytes each: it writes
    what it writes of any object it watches, namely which run of bits stepped as minutes do and how often, and which
    32-bit number kept rising and at what pace: the second reading of the time of day and of the play time.
  - What it gathered, for the panel: after each of your tasks the round tells the launcher one of two things. The
    load: a save was loaded and your character was there. The stand: your position kept still, and the sheet scan
    made two looks or more. The death: your health read nothing at some moment, and you were back with health when
    you pressed Pause. The stay: your character was in the world for half of it or more, the sheet scan went on, and
    the report as last written holds the background watch's own reading of the clock's minutes and of the play
    time's rise. The launcher shows it under the task, and writes it into its log.
  - Every two seconds it writes lodestar-found.json into %APPDATA%\EldenNet\probe, and lodestar-table.toml when
    another manager is named. This round's report may be up to 1 MiB. If it ever grows past that, it is written
    without the detail of the watched object that has the most, then of the next, and at the last with half of the
    longer list of the sheet's numbers, and says what was left out. If it cannot be written at all, the round says so
    in its log and on the panel. The files hold only numbers (offsets, sizes, counts, the jumps' numbers named above,
    your character's health, maximum health and save slot, and in this round the numbers of the sheet scan: your
    level, attributes, runes and count of deaths among them) and EldenNet's own labels: no names of the game's or of
    your character, no text, no paths, and nothing of your PC.
  - Watches the game until it ends, and keeps a short record if it ends badly: the error's code, which module it was
    in and the time, never a memory dump.


What it never does
------------------

  - It never calls anything in the game, never adds anything to the game's own lists, and never changes the game's
    code or data. It reads them.
  - It never goes online, and the game cannot reach FromSoftware's servers without Easy Anti-Cheat.
  - It never changes your saves or the game's files (steam_appid.txt aside, which it removes).
  - It never runs anything else, uses the network, keeps keys or sends anything anywhere. You send the files yourself.


If the game crashes or freezes
------------------------------

  - Your saves were backed up before the game started: each backup is a folder in %APPDATA%\EldenNet\save-backups,
    named by date and time, with a manifest of the files' sizes and hashes. The game's own save is not touched by
    EldenNet; only if the game itself reports a damaged save, copy ER0000.sl2 (and ER0000.sl2.bak) from the newest
    backup back into %APPDATA%\EldenRing\<the same number>\, with the game closed.
  - If the game freezes, close it with Alt+F4, or end eldenring.exe in Task Manager.
  - Send the files of step 5, the newest crash record included. The next start of the launcher explains it once,
    if the game was still on screen when it ended.
  - To play normally again, start the game from Steam as usual. Nothing of EldenNet stays loaded.


Check the files
---------------

In PowerShell, in this folder:

  Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 eldennet-launcher.exe, eldennet.dll, eldennet-engine.exe

Each value must equal its line in SHA256SUMS (letter case aside).


Safe use, and what we condemn
-----------------------------

The devkit's own words, as "eldennet-engine.exe probe-exe --help" prints them:

@DISCLOSURE@
